WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.426 --> 00:00:01.900
Film Courage: How should a writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:02.676
start a screenplay? Writer/Instructor&nbsp;
and Script Consultant Shannan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.676 --> 00:00:05.920
E. Johnson (The Professional Pen):
Oh! So there are several different ways

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.540
you can start a screenplay and a lot of
that goes back to whatever your idea is

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.150
there's probably thing that's going to
come to you first but you should

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:16.330
definitely then start trying to figure
out what all the rest of those elements

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:21.880
are so figuring out what the world is
knowing what the tone is a lot of people

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.890
may not think about tone but tone
matters

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:31.360
tone is that that that that that feeling
that makes this different from slapstick

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:38.800
comedy regular comedy romantic comedy
into kind of a comedic drama that is now

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.570
more regular drama family drama that
ends up now this gritty thriller thing

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:48.700
so it's kind of like the the nuance
between the genres right like what that

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:53.260
tone is and then knowing who your
characters are knowing who's gonna go on

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:58.000
this journey figuring out why we should
care about them figuring out how we're

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.180
gonna relate to them and relating to
them doesn't have to mean liking them we

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:05.680
just have to relate then knowing what
goal they're after because anything

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.810
that's on TV anything that's on anything
that's a feature is about a character

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:14.620
reaching a goal that's why we're here we
don't want to just see people go through

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:18.190
their day to day lives right we want to
see like their act or something or

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:22.510
something's after then you know whatever
it is but they have a goal figuring out

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.050
what the stakes are the stakes are gonna
be the why why am I here why do I care

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.690
what's what's going on what are they
gonna win what are they gonna lose

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:32.380
because if not then why do I care if
they reach the goal if they don't if

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:38.200
there's nothing at stake for them then I
am gonna be that much less invested and

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.500
people usually know what the end is
people usually kind of know what the

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.350
setup is and they kind of know what the
resolution is right

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:49.150
it's the second act where everyone kind
of falls apart and it's because we

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:54.970
really don't start acting our quick
asking ourselves how how are they gonna

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:59.650
get from A to B so I personally feel
like everyone should write an outline

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:03.100
they're gonna be writers who are reading
this like ha I've never written an

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.820
outline and I've done great that's great
you might have that special skills but

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:12.550
you know you also might be a person who
doesn't mind rewriting fifteen times

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:17.380
because no matter what you're going to
right right I think by writing the

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:21.130
outline and cut down the number of
rewrites because your first draft isn't

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:26.080
as terrible because you actually plotted
out what it is you're going to do so you

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.350
didn't just start at the blank page and
start writing and just like you know oh

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:32.110
well maybe they'll go this way maybe
they go that way because as soon as you

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.530
change one thing what does it do changes
everything else there's a domino effect

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:39.130
if you're doing that in outline form for
some people that's putting it on cards

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:42.070
some people that's putting it on a
whiteboard you know now you just erase

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:45.640
that one little part and write it write
it back or you move your your cards

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.370
around because you realize this scene
could probably be best there and it

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:51.640
heightens the stakes if we make this
thing happen here I do mine in a Word

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.570
document
and I put the actual scene heading on

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.930
top and then whatever is happening in
that scene so I can copy and paste that

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:03.370
whole little thing all over the place if
I want to so it's easier to kind of see

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:07.750
your story on paper and move it around
as much as you'd like before it gets

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:12.160
into script format it becomes a little
harder to do that so if you have an

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.270
outline and you really understand how
does this person get from A to C what

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:21.400
happens during B what do they physically
do so I'm always asking how and so

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.090
people will then answer that with with a
general answer and I'm gonna know I want

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.640
to know what the characters physically
doing like what am i watching them do

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:33.070
can you say well they're saving the
world how are they saving the world by

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:38.470
going to Starbucks are they saving the
world by having to go confront a bad guy

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:43.450
yes they have to confront the bad guy
how how did they find him where was he

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:47.230
was he just down the street you
know in another country he's in another

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:51.760
country how do they get there you know
do they take a bus do they take a train

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.810
do they have to fly do they swim you
know like all of these little details

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:59.950
about how we actually get from A to C
are the things that people don't think

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:03.820
about until they're on the page and then
they're stuck and they stop and they're

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.700
like I can't get past this part and it's
like yeah cuz you didn't think about

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:10.870
that part until you got here
but if you took some time to figure it

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:15.070
out before you get to the writing part
now the writing is fun because you got your

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:18.190
notes so you're like okay this is what's
supposed to be happening right now

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:23.140
okay I get it and now it can happen a
little more clean than it would have if

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.620
you just went straight to the page and
so now maybe instead of doing five

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:31.540
rewrites you only have to
you too so I teach a whole class called

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:35.800
no writer's block and that's what we do
we learn screenplay structure and by the

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.530
end of it you have an outline so that
you can go write whatever it is you want

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:41.620
to write also because you're learning
screenplay structure

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.160
now you can outline anything now you
know the structure of anything so it's

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:49.600
not just oh well I figured the outline
out for this idea now what it's like no

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.740
any idea can fall straight into that
same place so that you feel more

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:56.650
confident because that's the other
that's the where writer's block comes

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.590
from is that you just you're not
confident in what you're doing but if

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:03.460
you have a plan already right it's like
we were talking about earlier if I

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:08.620
already printed out my directions or if
I have my Google Maps on my phone then I

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:12.730
know where I'm going I say yes I can't
this is not a good example I was gonna

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:16.000
say I could say I want to drive to
Louisiana but if I don't look it up I

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.900
can't get there I happen to know where
Louisiana is from here so I could

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:23.380
probably get there but there are other
people who are like who would have no

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:28.900
idea that they can get on Interstate 10 and get
all the way to Louisiana some people

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:32.350
might not know that but so they would
need to have those directions if you

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:35.620
don't have those directions now you're
veering you know left and now you've

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:40.150
taken a wrong turn and now you've done X
Y &amp; Z and you've wasted however many

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.880
hours trying to get there when you could
add a straight shot it could just had

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:48.520
the directions so to me that's what an
outline is it's your Google Maps so that

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.730
you know where you're going so I think
beginning writers should definitely

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:55.810
start in that place well start by
learning structure right start by

00:05:55.810 --> 00:06:03.010
understanding what elements it takes to
have a story and then outline what if

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:10.240
they say well I'm good at outlining the
my first few but now I just know I don't

